      "publication_year": 2018,
      "publication_date": "2018-12-10",
      "abstract": "Postcolonial theorists have criticized the Frankfurt School for its reliance on forms of utopian thinking that build on teleological philosophies of history and Eurocentric narratives of progress.Although I am sympathetic to these concerns -voiced most powerfully by Amy Allen in The End of Progress (2016) -I contend that they overlook the extent to which the thinkers of the Frankfurt School theorize the past through the paradigm of collective memory, not stadial philosophies of history.This is true not only of the first generation of critical theory, but also of J\u00fcrgen Habermas, a frequent target of postcolonial critique.I argue that his recent work treats the past through the paradigm of collective memory as a fallible record of mistakes that should motivate social learning.Although Habermas and the Frankfurt School need to do more to extend these insights beyond Europe, their work contains the insights necessary to take this step.",
